-- title:
--   The parameter-computation program computes mkParams K N
-- statement:
--   Let $K, N \ge 1$ and $N < 2^{nb}$, and let $s$ be a machine state whose register `rN` holds $N$ and `rNb` holds $nb$ on top. Then the straight-line register program `parP K` is defined on $s$ and its result is `parOut s K N`: the state $s$ with the parameters of $q=\mathrm{mkParams}(K,N)$ pushed on their registers — $b$, $L$, $E = \lceil \log_2 4L \rceil$, $e$, $d_h$, $K-d_h$, $2^e$, $\alpha$, $p$, $P$, $f_n$, the list of side lengths $s_1,\dots,s_K$ (as records) and their product $S$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** `RS` is straight-line code on the 64 item stacks with `run`/`ok`; `pushV` pushes an integer on a stack. `mkParams` is from `Def_IntMul_HvdH_MachineRef`. The machine layer: `IState HS` is a state with $64$ stacks of items (`Item` = integer or mark), an input position and an output; `RM` programs (straight-line register code `RS`, loops `rep`, conditionals, calls of the multiplication procedure through a `MulSpec` $M$ giving its domain and cost) have a functional semantics `p.run s`, a definedness predicate `p.ok M s` and a cost `p.cost M s`; `Spec M p s s' c` abbreviates `p.ok M s ∧ p.run s = s' ∧ p.cost M s ≤ c`. `ints L` / `gints U` push an integer list / a list of pairs as items (head = top); `zsz z` is the bit length of $|z|$. `MulBnd M m Φ` says the multiplication procedure accepts all pairs of integers of bit length $\le m$ at cost $\le Φ$. Register names (`GU`, `CR`, `KB`, `XN`, …) are fixed stack indices.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.CR.parP_spec` of the Lean development NLogN/HCrPar3.lean (lines 657–677), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

